# CheckMed — change discipline

Protect the working Dashboard. Do not rewrite architecture, navigation, auth, or dose/adherence unless the feature truly requires it. Touch only the systems the feature needs.

Before any change, know: file, why, what can break, what persists, which views observe it, whether it can loop SwiftUI, whether it can run twice, whether it is idempotent.

Prefer the smallest safe change. No extra managers, stores, models, or global notifications unless required.

## Bugs

Reproduce → targeted diagnostics → prove the mutation/call path → smallest fix → re-test the bug and existing flows. Do not guess. Do not hide races with delays.

After significant work, verify Home is interactive (tabs, scroll, +, Treatments, Refill, Profile), data survives relaunch/foreground, login/logout, and Home does **not** continuously rebuild.

## Launch, tasks, MainActor

One launch path: restore auth → restore data → mark ready → **one** navigation decision. Empty/loading is not “new user.” Multiple systems must not independently decide Home vs new vs unfinished.

Every `.task` / `onAppear` / `onChange`: why, how often, what it writes, whether that redraws and re-enters, cancellation. Do not add observed state to Home that changes frequently unless Home must show it.

Do not dump large SwiftData scans, OCR, PDF, or sync onto MainActor inside UI. Use Swift concurrency carefully; do not sprinkle `Task.detached` as a workaround.

If a request would violate these rules, stop and explain the conflict before editing.
